// The feel contracts of docs/design/progression.md section 7 as bands the
// harness checks: `just balance-check`, and the `contracts` test of
// tools/balance. A band names a preset matchup (`balance list`), a metric
// of its summary row, and the range the metric must lie in over `bouts`
// bouts from `seed`. A band with `chosen: true` is the design's range and
// a miss fails the check. A band with `chosen: false` is "measured, not
// yet chosen": where the shipped tables put the number on the day the
// band was written, not where the design wants it; a miss is printed and
// does not fail, the wish is in the rationale, and the band moves when
// the tables do, through the ledger.
//
// Metrics: WinRate (side a's), DrawRate, MeanTime (decided bouts), A(x) and
// B(x) for a side's book (Dealt, Taken, DealtPerSecond, Shots, Hits,
// HitShare, Cuts, CutsLanded, CutShare, StaminaMin, PostureMin, Breaks,
// Dodges, DodgesAvoided, Blocks, Parries, GuardBreaks, Abilities), and Relative(metric: ..., control: "<preset>")
// for the metric over the same metric of the control matchup.
Contracts(
    version: 1,
    bouts: 20,
    seed: 1,
    // The dodge pair runs to the cap (a player who only dodges kills no
    // one), and the check runs in debug under `just test`, so the cap is
    // what the bands need: the bracing control falls by about 28 s.
    cap: 30.0,
    bands: [
        // Measured, not yet chosen. The design wants 8 to 14 s; on open
        // ground with an exact lead two carbines empty 140 pools into each
        // other in six hits, 1.75 s on 2026-10-01 and again on 2026-10-11
        // with the wider cones, 1.76 s with the wider ones of the follow-up (both
        // sides read the table's row).
        Band(
            name: "fair-duel-length",
            matchup: "carbine-vs-carbine",
            metric: MeanTime,
            low: 1.4,
            high: 2.1,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: the design wants a fair duel between equal players to last 8 to 14 s, long enough to read and answer and short enough that a mistake decides it; with the cones of step 11 and its follow-up the tables still gave under 2 s (1.76 s on 2026-10-11), and with the carbine's bursts of ticket 0010 step 1 it is 1.59 s on 2026-10-12; the band records that until the tables are tuned.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "pistol-rate-share",
            matchup: "pistol-vs-dummy",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy"),
            // Measured, not yet chosen. A range of 0.70 to 0.90 is proposed
            // here, not stated in the design; the two weapons share a
            // nominal rate (24 per 0.24 s and 15 per 0.15 s) and only the
            // pistol's reload separates them: 0.95 on 2026-10-01 and again on 2026-10-11, against the table's
            // carbine row, and 0.88 on 2026-10-11 with the follow-up's wider
            // pistol cone.
            low: 0.70,
            high: 1.06,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: proposed, and not in the design, is that the pistol, the sidearm, kills a standing target at 70 to 90 percent of the carbine's rate, slower but not so slow that drawing it is never right; with the guns' roles of ticket 0010 step 1 the pistol, one single shot a press, kills a standing target at 12 m at 0.77 of the carbine's bursts on 2026-10-12, and the band records that until a range is chosen and the tables tuned.",
        ),
        // Measured, not yet chosen. The bought carbine reads its own action
        // line, 1.10 of the table's interval (item 4 of step 11), so the
        // same shooter on the same ground kills a standing target a little
        // slower; the ratio dilutes toward 1 by the bolt's flight, which
        // does not change. The row above, `carbine-vs-dummy`, is the table's
        // carbine, which no weapon in play reads.
        Band(
            name: "a-bought-carbine-is-slower",
            matchup: "bought-carbine-vs-dummy",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy"),
            low: 0.75,
            high: 0.98,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: a store-grade carbine fires at 1.10 of the table's interval, so it kills a standing target measurably slower than the table's carbine, 0.96 on 2026-10-12 (a bought weapon that does nothing against the table reads exactly 1.00, and fails); the design wants a bought weapon clearly worse than a well made one.",
        ),
        // Measured, not yet chosen. A line of 800 is a factor of 0.98 of the
        // table's interval (the signature runs 1.30 at 0 to 0.90 at 1000),
        // 0.89 of a bought carbine's 1.10; 800 is what a smith can make (a conductor of
        // 900 and 800 is a harness of 880, with a frame of 800 stability
        // an Action of 840), where 900 was out of reach.
        Band(
            name: "a-made-carbine-is-faster",
            matchup: "made-carbine-vs-dummy",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "bought-carbine-vs-dummy"),
            low: 1.02,
            high: 1.31,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: a carbine made to 800 on its action line (a harness of 880 from a surveyed conductor of Conductivity 900 and Purity 800, with a frame of Stability 800 gives 840) fires at 0.98 of the table's interval, 0.89 of a bought one's, and measured 1.09 of a bought one's rate on 2026-10-11 and 1.05 on 2026-10-12 with the burst pause of ticket 0010 step 1, so it kills a standing target faster than the bought carbine (no change reads exactly 1.00, and fails); the crafted weapon is meant to reward the player's work.",
        ),
        // Measured, not yet chosen. The trio stands at 27 m, the sentry's
        // range: with the aimed cone at 2.0 degrees (0.94 m there) the
        // unsighted carbine lands about half its bolts, so the sight shows at
        // the range the game fights at; nearer, the cone lands every bolt. A
        // bolt is in the air for most of a second, so the pair is read on
        // time to kill, not on bolts. Each bout draws its own stretch of the
        // cone (its seed salts the shot count), and the trio shares seeds.
        Band(
            name: "a-sight-tightens-aimed-fire",
            matchup: "sighted-carbine-vs-dummy-27m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "aimed-carbine-vs-dummy-27m"),
            low: 1.15,
            high: 1.73,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: a reflex sight at store grade tightens aimed spread by 30 percent, so from the sentry's range an aimed shooter tapping bursts kills a standing target measurably faster than the same shooter without it (a sight that does nothing reads exactly 1.00 on the shared draws, and fails); the sight's worth is only in misses it prevents, which is why the trio stands at 27 m, where the unsighted cone lands only part of its bolts, and the pair measured 1.44 on 2026-10-11 and 1.47 on 2026-10-12.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "a-fine-sight-beats-a-store-sight",
            matchup: "fine-sighted-carbine-vs-dummy-27m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "sighted-carbine-vs-dummy-27m"),
            low: 1.01,
            high: 1.32,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: a reflex sight whose lines are 900 narrows the aimed cone to 1.1 degrees against the store sight's 1.40, so the same shooter kills a standing target faster with it, 1.10 measured on 2026-10-11 and 1.22 on 2026-10-12 (the same sight at store grade reads exactly 1.00, and fails); a part's quality has to count (no schematic makes a sight yet, so the sight stands in for any banded part until one can be made).",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "blade-closes-on-carbine",
            matchup: "blade-vs-carbine-12m",
            metric: WinRate,
            // Measured, not yet chosen. A coin toss, 0.35 to 0.65, is
            // proposed here, not stated in the design; the blade needs
            // 2.2 s to cross 12 m at actor.walk_speed and the carbine kills
            // in 1.6, so the blade never arrives: 0.00 on 2026-10-01 and again on 2026-10-11.
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.10,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen: proposed, and not in the design, is that a blade closing and chaining from 12 m against a carbine that holds and fires is a coin toss, 35 to 65 percent, so neither weapon is the answer everywhere; today's tables never let the blade arrive (0.00 again on 2026-10-12) and the band records that until a range is chosen and the tables tuned.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "dodge-earns-its-stamina",
            matchup: "dodge-vs-sentry-12m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(Taken), control: "brace-vs-sentry-12m"),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.70,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "Dodging on the sentry's charge, 0.15 to 0.45 s after it shows, takes at least 30 percent less damage than bracing on the same ground without it, or the dodge is not worth learning. The pair neither fires nor strafes: a player who fires kills the game's sentry before its first charge ends, and its bolt is aimed where the player was when the charge began, so a strafing control is never hit; either way a ratio of nothing to nothing says nothing.",
        ),
        // The guard (ticket 0010, step 2b): the aim button with a melee weapon in
        // hand. The sentry pair closes from 12 m behind a raised guard, against
        // the bracing control above; the rusher pair is a blocking blade and a
        // parrying one (the guard down until a blow is about 0.15 s from
        // landing); the carbine pair is the guard against a gun from 25 m.
        Band(
            name: "guard-earns-its-stamina",
            matchup: "guard-vs-sentry-12m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(Taken), control: "brace-vs-sentry-12m"),
            low: 0.2,
            high: 0.75,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "A blade closing on the sentry from 12 m and standing behind a raised guard (it does not cut, as the dodge's pair neither fires nor strafes, so the sentry lives and both sides take bolts for the same stretch) takes at most three quarters of the damage the bracing control takes on the same ground, and not so little that the guard makes the walk free (the floor, 0.2): the guard has to be worth its stamina and its slow pace. Measured at the check's 30 s cap: on a longer table the bracing control dies and the ratio climbs, so this band reads the share dial and the walk, not a fight to the end.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "a-parry-turns-the-rusher",
            matchup: "blade-parry-vs-rusher",
            metric: ParryShare,
            low: 0.15,
            high: 0.60,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "A blade that keeps the guard down until the rusher's Heavy is 0.15 s from landing parries between 15 and 60 in a hundred of the Heavies the rusher begins. It could pass at 0 or at 1 with the old win-rate form (the blade wins the rusher's fight with or without a guard); this form fails if the parry is never landed, and fails if it is always landed, which would make the reaction of the policy the whole fight. The rusher's cooldown is 2.6 s, a fight about 5 s. At 20 bouts it reads 0.17 to 0.26 over three seeds, near its floor: four to seven parries.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "a-parry-costs-less-than-no-guard",
            matchup: "blade-parry-vs-rusher",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(Taken), control: "blade-vs-rusher"),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.60,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "Both blades win the rusher's fight, so the parry is read in what it costs: the parrying blade takes at most 0.6 of what the unguarded blade takes (the rusher's Heavy is 27.5 of 140 to the unguarded blade).",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "a-parry-beats-a-block",
            matchup: "blade-parry-vs-rusher",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(Taken), control: "blade-guard-vs-rusher"),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.9,
            chosen: true,
            bouts: Some(60),
            rationale: "The same blade takes less from the rusher when it parries than when it only blocks, or the window is not worth the timing. Read from 60 bouts (step 9): on seeded cutting hands the ratio of two takings spreads from 0.22 to 1.10 over 20 bout windows and holds at 0.29 to 0.43 over four 60 bout ones.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "a-parry-is-landed-against-the-rusher",
            matchup: "blade-parry-vs-rusher",
            metric: A(Parries),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 10.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: how many of the rusher's Heavies the parrying blade turns in a bout, so the riposte is read and not only the win. The parry is late a share of the time (the lead is drawn per blow), so some of the Heavies are blocked.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "guarded-closer-against-a-carbine",
            matchup: "blade-guard-vs-carbine-25m",
            metric: WinRate,
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.10,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the wish is that a melee weapon against a gun from 25 m is a fair fight once the closing moves of a later step exist; today the guarded closer, which blocks every bolt it can, still never arrives.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "unguarded-closer-against-a-carbine",
            matchup: "blade-vs-carbine-25m",
            metric: WinRate,
            low: 0.0,
            high: 0.10,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the control of the band above, the same closer with no guard; the wish is the same, and today it never arrives either.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "chain-lands-on-a-standing-target",
            matchup: "chain-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(CutShare),
            low: 0.90,
            high: 1.0,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "Light, its return cut and a Heavy land on a target that does not move; a chain that whiffs on a dummy is a motor fault, not a skill test.",
        ),
        // The boxed pairs: a side with boxes is fitted at spawn through
        // the game's own merge, so a band here reads a box, not a table.
        Band(
            name: "cuts-3-lands-the-heavy",
            matchup: "heavy-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(CutShare),
            low: 0.90,
            high: 1.0,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "A sheet with the novice and One hand 1 to 3 (the Heavy is the novice's since ticket 0010 step 7, and One hand 3 gives it 1.15 of its posture) that swings only Heavies at a standing target begins them and lands them: a Heavy that whiffs on a dummy is a motor fault. It is no longer the rusher's sheet (the game's rusher knows One hand 1 and 2 and swings the table's Heavy, step 8); it measures that a box that scales the Heavy leaves it landing.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "blade-master-beats-novice",
            matchup: "blade-master-vs-novice",
            metric: WinRate,
            low: 0.80,
            high: 1.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Step 9c: the same whole tree played as a master would (blade-master-guard-vs-novice: GuardAndCut with parry, heavy cut, thrust and disarm on its bar) wins 0.02, 0.05 and 0.03 of 60 bouts at seeds 1, 101 and 501 and takes 140 of 140: the guarding hand loses to the novice's cutting hand, so the tree is not shown to carry a master past a novice by either hand and the band stays unchosen; the boxes that read as worth nothing were not enumerated. Unchosen at step 9: the master wins about one bout in two, 0.46 over four 60 bout windows (0.38 to 0.52; 0.35 to 0.65 over three 20 bout ones), where it read 1.00 only because a melee bout was one trajectory whatever the seed. The wish is unchanged: the whole Blade tree, 98 points, against the novice's sheet (the novice box and Cuts 1 to 3, the rusher's, so both sides have the full moveset and the other fourteen boxes are the difference) wins at least four bouts in five from 8 m with the same policy on both sides; a master the boxes do not carry past a novice is a tree that is not worth its points. A finding for the next step. Why: GuardAndCut meets 0.30 blocks and 0.88 parries of the novice's 6.2 cuts and makes 1.25 cuts of its own in 3.6 s, so its guard boxes are barely read; it does not hold a chain-cutting novice. The cutting master, with no guard, wins 0.57. Naming the boxes of no marginal worth needs a `balance sweep` on blade-master-vs-novice, not done.",
        ),
        // The guns' ranges (ticket 0010, step 1): a shooter standing and aiming
        // at the passive dummy, the pistol tapping and the carbine bursting,
        // read on how fast each kills. Each gun owns a range and loses
        // outside it; the held trigger is for arm's length.
        Band(
            name: "pistol-trails-the-carbine-up-close",
            matchup: "pistol-vs-dummy-10m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy-10m"),
            low: 0.55,
            high: 0.90,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 10 m the carbine's burst kills a standing target faster than the pistol's single shots: the pistol deals 55 to 90 percent of the carbine's damage a second, so the carbine is the gun for a close fight.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "pistol-matches-the-carbine-at-mid-range",
            matchup: "pistol-vs-dummy-30m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy-30m"),
            low: 1.00,
            high: 1.60,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 30 m the carbine's cone has spread and its damage has begun to fade while the pistol's has not: the pistol deals 1.0 to 1.6 times the carbine's damage a second, so the pistol is the medium range gun.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "pistol-owns-long-range",
            matchup: "pistol-vs-dummy-60m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy-60m"),
            low: 1.30,
            high: 4.0,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 60 m the carbine's bolts have faded to half and scatter wide: the pistol deals at least 1.3 times the carbine's damage a second. The band stops at 4, because a carbine that does nothing at all at 60 m would be a range the pistol owns by default, not by design.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "full-auto-wins-at-arms-length",
            matchup: "carbine-hold-vs-dummy-8m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-burst-vs-dummy-8m"),
            low: 1.15,
            high: 1.80,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 8 m a trigger held past the burst deals 1.15 to 1.8 times the damage a second of bursts: full auto is worth the cone it costs for a rusher at arm's length.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "full-auto-loses-at-distance",
            matchup: "carbine-hold-vs-dummy-30m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "carbine-vs-dummy-30m"),
            low: 0.2,
            high: 0.90,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 30 m a held trigger deals at most 0.9 of the damage a second of bursts: the cone it grows wastes the rounds, so full auto is the wrong answer at range.",
        ),
        // The rifle (ticket 0010, step 4): the long gun. It owns the range past
        // 50 m, holds it, is the worst of the three up close and is nothing
        // without its aim. The shooter stands and taps at the row's own
        // interval; the hip shooter moves about at 30 m. The better of the
        // pistol and the carbine at 60 m is the pistol (the band above holds
        // it at 1.3 times the carbine), so it is the control there.
        Band(
            name: "the-rifle-owns-the-long-range",
            matchup: "rifle-vs-dummy-60m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "pistol-vs-dummy-60m"),
            low: 4.0,
            high: 12.0,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 60 m the pistol's bolts have faded and scatter and the carbine's are spent: the rifle deals at least 4 times the damage a second of the better of the two, so past 50 m it is the only gun worth firing.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "the-rifle-holds-its-range",
            matchup: "rifle-vs-dummy-120m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "rifle-vs-dummy-60m"),
            low: 0.70,
            high: 1.10,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "The rifle's damage does not fade inside its full distance and its aimed cone is a few centimetres at 120 m: it deals at least 0.7 of its 60 m rate there, the rest being the bolt's longer flight.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "the-rifle-is-the-worst-close",
            matchup: "rifle-vs-dummy-10m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "pistol-vs-dummy-10m"),
            low: 0.30,
            high: 0.85,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "At 10 m a rifle shot is slow and its cone takes time to close: it deals at most 0.85 of the pistol's damage a second, and the pistol deals less than the carbine's there (the band above), so the rifle is the worst of the three close in.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "the-rifle-needs-its-aim",
            matchup: "rifle-hip-vs-dummy-30m",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "rifle-vs-dummy-30m"),
            low: 0.00,
            high: 0.40,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "A rifle fired from the hip on the move at 30 m, its cone wide, deals at most 0.4 of what the same rifle deals standing with its aim closed: it is tight only when aimed and still.",
        ),
        // The melee classes on a training post (ticket 0010, step 2), each
        // standing at its own reach and cutting the chain with a Heavy every
        // third opening, waiting at a Heavy opening until stamina covers it.
        // The post has 300 health, so three openings are always swung and the
        // Heavy is read. Measured, not yet chosen, on 2026-10-12 (the first
        // half's figures, taken against the 100 health dummy where no Heavy
        // was ever swung, were 72 to 94 damage a second).
        Band(
            name: "blade-damage-rate",
            matchup: "blade-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(DealtPerSecond),
            low: 34.0,
            high: 43.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the damage a second the Blade's cut rows give today against a post of 300 health, with the Heavy swung at every third opening once stamina covers it (the opening waits for it).",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "blade-stamina-low",
            matchup: "blade-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(StaminaMin),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 2.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the lowest stamina the Blade reaches against the post: the policy spends it to the last point, for a Heavy opening waits only until stamina covers the cut.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "greatblade-damage-rate",
            matchup: "greatblade-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(DealtPerSecond),
            low: 29.0,
            high: 36.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the damage a second the Greatblade's cut rows give today against a post of 300 health, with the Heavy swung at every third opening once stamina covers it (the opening waits for it).",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "greatblade-stamina-low",
            matchup: "greatblade-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(StaminaMin),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 2.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the lowest stamina the Greatblade reaches against the post: the policy spends it to the last point, for a Heavy opening waits only until stamina covers the cut.",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "glaive-damage-rate",
            matchup: "glaive-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(DealtPerSecond),
            low: 26.5,
            high: 33.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the damage a second the Glaive's cut rows give today against a post of 300 health, with the Heavy swung at every third opening once stamina covers it (the opening waits for it).",
        ),
        Band(
            name: "glaive-stamina-low",
            matchup: "glaive-vs-dummy",
            metric: A(StaminaMin),
            low: 0.0,
            high: 2.0,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-12: the design wants the Greatblade to deal the most posture damage a second and the least damage a second, and the Glaive to land on the post from further than the Blade can; this band records the lowest stamina the Glaive reaches against the post: the policy spends it to the last point, for a Heavy opening waits only until stamina covers the cut.",
        ),
        // The made blade (ticket 0010, step 3): the service blade's edge line
        // is a signature on its hit damage, 0.80 at nought to 1.10 at a
        // thousand, so a bought blade (500) cuts at 0.95 of the class's
        // figure and one made to 900 at 1.07, 1.126 a cut; the bout is a
        // whole number of cuts, so the rate moves in steps. Both stand at
        // the Blade's reach against the 300 health post, waiting for a
        // Heavy's stamina.
        Band(
            name: "a-made-edge-shows",
            matchup: "made-blade-vs-dummy",
            metric: Relative(metric: A(DealtPerSecond), control: "bought-blade-vs-dummy"),
            low: 1.08,
            high: 1.40,
            chosen: true,
            rationale: "A blade made to 900 on its edge line, which a smith with Edges 1 and a hard metal can reach, kills the post faster than the bought blade does, by at least the 1.08 a player can feel (the design: crafting must pay). Measured 1.247 on 2026-10-12. The rate moves in whole cuts: a cut gains 1.126, and the 1.247 is the made blade needing eight cuts where the bought one needs nine, with a stamina wait saved. The high leaves room for that step, so a neighbouring dial that moves the count does not fail a band whose point is the floor.",
        ),
        // Measured, not yet chosen. The post's lowest posture is the book's
        // measure of posture damage dealt: the lower it goes the harder the
        // blade hit it. The hooked edge carries 1.08 on hit posture and 1.06 on
        // the tail of every cut, so it breaks the post's guard harder and
        // swings a little slower: the bout ran 11.21 s against the bought
        // blade's 11.06 (a damage rate of 0.99 of it) on 2026-10-12.
        Band(
            name: "a-hooked-edge-breaks-posture",
            matchup: "hooked-blade-vs-dummy",
            metric: Relative(metric: B(PostureMin), control: "bought-blade-vs-dummy"),
            low: 1.05,
            high: 1.60,
            chosen: false,
            rationale: "Measured, not yet chosen, 2026-10-13 (caught rule, step 5c, moved it from 1.21 to 1.37; the high was 1.40): a store grade hooked edge on a bought blade pushes the post's lowest posture to -72.8 from -60, 1.21 of the unfitted blade's depth (both are below nought, so the ratio is of their depths), at a recovery cost of 1.06 on the tail of each cut that stretched the bout from 11.06 s to 11.21 s. The design wants a part to be a choice with a price, and this is the price.",
        ),
        // The active skills (ticket 0010, step 5): a side that knows an
        // ability presses it by the sim's own rule whenever its condition
        // holds (the harness's PressAbilities), beside the same fight
        // without it. The first two are chosen: crafting and a bar slot pay.
